window.AXIORA_SEARCH = [{"title": "Overview", "url": "index.html", "description": "The design at a glance and reading paths.", "text": "The Axiora handbook Axiora is a proposed offline learning environment for proof-based mathematics. It pairs a native Android study application with a separate content toolchain. This handbook develops the idea into a bounded, implementable design. **Status: design baseline 0.1 · 16 November 2026.** Requirements below are intended contracts, not claims about existing software. Implemented parts include this documentation site and its checks, an illustrative package fixture, the Kotlin content model and validator, a deterministic package CLI, and the headless study/review engine. The JVM SQLite repository, package installer, migration, and backup/restore adapter also pass Phase 4 acceptance checks. Android has not started; see the [verified implementation record](../../IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md). Start with the learning problem A learner can recite a definition and still fail to recognize its examples. They can follow a proof and still be unable to start one. Axiora should make these differences visible and provide useful next steps. It should gradually reduce assistance as the learner becomes more capable. The first release will test that premise with one carefully authored course slice. Broad curriculum coverage, arbitrary textbook conversion, and a conversational tutor come later, if the small version proves useful. Reading paths Your question Read What are we making, for whom, and what counts as success? [Product and scope](product.md) How should an actual study session teach? [Learning and assessment](pedagogy.md) How are the components separated? [System architecture](architecture.md) What must a package and teaching unit contain? [Content contract](content.md) How are attempts, progress, and reviews preserved? [Learner state](learner-state.md) How does source material become usable content? [Authoring and compilation](compiler.md) What does the Android experience feel like? [Study experience](experience.md) What should be implemented first, and how is it accepted? [Delivery and verification](delivery.md) Why were these choices made? [Decision log](decisions.md) For AI-assisted implementation, read the [repository instructions](../../AGENTS.md), then the relevant contracts. The [example package](../../examples/prime-and-maximal/README.md) makes the content model concrete. Four things that must remain separate Entity Meaning Example Concept Mathematical knowledge, under stated conventions Prime ideal in a commutative ring with identity Learning unit A bounded teaching experience Find why prime does not imply maximal Package A versioned distribution of content A course slice containing several units Learner record A person's work and observations over time An attempted proof, hints used, and later review A concept can appear in many units. A unit can teach several concepts. Packages assemble units. Learner records refer to stable content identifiers without becoming part of the package. Design commitments The core study loop works offline, without login or runtime AI. Content is declarative data with explicit limits and compatibility rules. Structured answers can be checked only within a declared evaluator's domain. Open proofs use rubrics and learner review in the first release. Completion, correctness, verification, and durable learning are separate signals. Content updates retain historical work and never silently reinterpret old evidence. How to read normative language **Must** describes a required invariant or acceptance condition. **Should** describes a default that may be changed with a recorded reason. **May** describes an optional capability. “Deferred” means outside the first release. Numerical limits and review intervals are baseline product policies to evaluate, not empirically established optimal values. The detailed Markdown is authoritative. HTML is generated from those same files. Changing a contract should update its example, linked decisions, and acceptance criteria in the same change."}, {"title": "Product & scope", "url": "product.html", "description": "Who it serves, what it promises, and where the first release stops.", "text": "Product and scope The product promise Help an independent learner build mathematical understanding and proof ability through structured work they can resume anywhere. Axiora should make the next useful task clear while leaving room to explore, write, and think for an extended period. Its distinctive mechanism is the combination of reusable teaching units, explicit evidence about different skills, and an offline study engine. AI is an optional production or assistance tool rather than the organizing principle of the learner experience. Primary learner The initial design assumes an adult studying undergraduate or early graduate proof-based mathematics. They know basic mathematical notation, have limited access to a tutor, and use an Android phone or tablet. Their connectivity may be intermittent. They may alternate between a textbook, paper, and the app. This is a design hypothesis to test with real learners. Axiora has no validated user research or demonstrated learning outcomes yet. The initial course assumes comfort with elementary ring theory. Prerequisite diagnostics should reveal gaps in ideals, quotient rings, integral domains, and fields. This is not a course that teaches all of those topics from zero. Jobs to support Situation Learner's job Useful outcome Opening an unfamiliar topic Discover what matters and what is assumed A clear objective and a useful first question Confusing two definitions Explore examples and boundary cases State the distinction and construct an example Unable to start a proof Plan an approach with limited support Produce an attempt before seeing a solution Returning after a gap Recover prior knowledge Retrieve an idea and apply it again Studying without connectivity Continue the same work No blocked lesson, missing formula, or lost attempt A complete first release The first release contains a single manually reviewed course slice: **prime and maximal ideals**. It includes a short prerequisite check, examples and nonexamples, a counterexample to a false implication, a guided proof of the valid implication, an independent attempt, and delayed review. The committed example is a smaller contract fixture inside that slice. It does not by itself constitute the full release curriculum. Required capabilities: Import a validated package from device storage and inspect its identity, conventions, prerequisites, and review status. Resume a deterministic branching unit with exact-choice evaluation. Write a plain-text proof with LaTeX notation, save it locally, and compare it with a model solution and rubric. Record hints and assistance without pretending the resulting work was independent. Schedule simple reviews and allow manual topic selection. Preserve notes, attempts, and historical results across an application restart and compatible content update. Export and restore learner records through an explicit local file action. The first release uses a small vocabulary of activities. Rich symbolic equivalence checking and automatic free-text grading are not prerequisites. Out of scope initially No cloud accounts, synchronization, content marketplace, teacher dashboard, leaderboards, streak economy, handwriting recognition, collaborative editing, arbitrary code execution, formal theorem proving, or real-time conversational tutor. No promise to convert an arbitrary PDF into a correct course automatically. There is no commitment to support every branch of mathematics with the initial evaluator set. A general content model must not become a reason to build a general computer algebra system. What success means Evaluate three different things: Dimension Evidence to collect What it cannot establish alone Usability Learners import, resume, request a hint, and recover saved work Mathematical learning Reasoning A human reviewer compares independent attempts against a stated rubric Long-term retention Retention and transfer Learners solve delayed, previously unseen tasks with reduced help Effectiveness across all topics or populations Pilot design: recruit a small number of willing learners, ask them to complete a session and return after a delay, and inspect where their reasoning and the interface break down. Sample size, consent wording, and any research design remain open. Do not publish efficacy claims from anecdotal completion rates. The first pilot's product gate is practical: can learners complete and resume the loop, understand the assessment labels, and produce enough work for meaningful human feedback? A later controlled evaluation is needed for causal learning claims. Operating principles **Make effort meaningful.** A substantial proof attempt can be a better session than many fast answers. Offer short and extended sessions without penalizing either. **Make uncertainty visible.** Show “compared with rubric” or “answer checked” rather than a universal “verified” badge. **Make recovery ordinary.** Missing prerequisites, corrupt imports, incompatible updates, and unfinished arguments are expected states with useful next actions. **Keep autonomy.** Recommendations explain their reason and can be overridden. The learner may inspect definitions, revisit work, or stop a session. Open product questions Tablet versus phone priority, target Android devices, additional languages, distribution model, software license, content licensing, and final name availability remain owner decisions. None blocks documenting or prototyping the offline loop."}, {"title": "Learning & assessment", "url": "pedagogy.html", "description": "Teaching, proof practice, assistance, and honest evidence.", "text": "Learning and assessment Design a session around a capability Each unit should name a small observable objective, such as “distinguish prime from maximal ideals using a counterexample.” Avoid objectives expressed only as exposure: “read the definition” is an activity, not evidence of understanding. A useful session begins with a question, surfaces the learner's reasoning, offers an appropriate intervention, and ends with a fresh opportunity to act. The exact sequence depends on the mathematics. Do not impose one universal explanation–quiz template. A teaching sequence for the first slice **Retrieve prerequisites.** Ask what makes a quotient a field or an integral domain. Offer a brief reminder when needed. **Commit to a prediction.** Is every prime ideal maximal? Record the initial answer before revealing feedback. **Investigate a boundary case.** Examine the zero ideal in the integers. Require a justification, not just another selection. **Analyze the valid direction.** Explain why a maximal ideal is prime using quotient characterizations, under the stated ring conventions. **Attempt a proof.** Let the learner plan and write before opening hints or the solution. **Compare and revise.** Use a rubric to locate missing hypotheses, invalid inferences, and unexplained steps. **Return later.** Present a different example or proof task to test retrieval and transfer. The package fixture demonstrates steps 2–6 in miniature. It deliberately does not claim that one question and one self-reviewed proof establish mastery. Track distinct skills Skill key Observable capability Example evidence `recall` Retrieve an accurate statement State the definition without the reference open `recognition` Identify examples and nonexamples Classify an ideal and justify the classification `application` Use a result under its hypotheses Choose a quotient characterization appropriately `proof_comprehension` Explain an argument's structure Identify why a field is an integral domain `proof_construction` Build a coherent argument Prove maximal implies prime `transfer` Apply an idea in a new setting Find a counterexample in an unfamiliar ring Self-explanation is a teaching activity across these skills, not an automatic scoring method. The first release may display skill-specific observations without calculating a single aggregate mastery percentage. Evaluation is not mastery An attempt has independent dimensions: **Outcome:** `correct`, `incorrect`, `partial`, or `unassessed`. **Method:** `exact_choice`, `self_review`, `human_review`, `symbolic`, `formal`, or `ai_feedback`. **Assistance:** which hints, references, and solutions were opened, and when. **Evidence:** objective, skill, content revision, and assessment family. Only `exact_choice` and `self_review` are required in the first release. Other methods are reserved for later contracts, not accepted first-release evaluator types. An exact-choice evaluator checks agreement with the authored key. It does not establish that the learner can prove the claim or that the authored key is mathematically correct. Mathematical author review is a separate process. An unsupported response must remain unassessed. A technical error, blank draft, or interrupted submission is not a mathematical error. An assessment may record a misconception as a hypothesis associated with an authored distractor; it must not infer a permanent learner diagnosis from one response. Proofs without automatic proof grading The learner writes an attempt, then explicitly chooses “Compare with solution.” The app snapshots the attempt and reveals the model argument and rubric. Each criterion is marked `met`, `not_met`, or `unsure` by the learner. The resulting method is `self_review`, and the proof outcome remains `unassessed`. Example rubric for maximal implies prime: State that the ideal is proper and the ambient ring is commutative with identity. Use that the quotient by a maximal ideal is a field. Explain that a field is an integral domain. Apply the quotient characterization of prime ideals and conclude the required implication. Alternative valid direct proofs must be acknowledged in the rubric guidance. Matching the model solution's wording is never a criterion. Rubric uncertainty should lead to a reminder, a second attempt, or human feedback; it must not become a hidden failure score. Hints as support, not penalties Hints should progress from a relevant definition to an intermediate goal to a structural outline. A complete solution is a separate reveal action. The app records each reveal but never shames or withholds essential content. Assistance affects the interpretation of evidence. An answer after a revealed solution is useful practice but cannot qualify as an independent retrieval success. A later fresh, unassisted task can provide new evidence. Evidence policy for the first release Use descriptive states per objective and skill: `unseen`: no eligible observation. `practicing`: observations exist, but independence or consistency is insufficient. `demonstrated`: at least two first-submission correct exact-choice observations from distinct assessment families, on distinct local study dates at least 24 hours apart, with no hints, reference reveals, or solutions shown before submission. `revisit`: a subsequent eligible first-submission error, or a passed review date, indicates more practice is useful. Only skills covered by the evaluator can enter `demonstrated`. In version 0.1, exact-choice evidence is restricted to recall and recognition. Proof construction remains descriptively tracked through attempts and rubric reflections; it cannot enter `demonstrated` under this policy. The 24-hour/two-family rule is an initial engineering heuristic, not a validated mastery model. Keep the policy version in projections so it can change without rewriting attempts. Do not treat a device clock as a trusted measure of elapsed learning time. Content quality rubric Before a unit is included in a pilot, a mathematical reviewer should check conventions, hypotheses, examples, keys, model solutions, and rubric flexibility. A teaching reviewer should check whether the tasks elicit the stated skill, whether distractors are informative, and whether assistance actually helps. A useful unit needs variation, a plausible misconception, an explanatory intervention, and a fresh check. The first release should prefer a few deeply reviewed units over broad generated coverage. No external empirical efficacy claims are assumed by this design."}, {"title": "Architecture", "url": "architecture.html", "description": "Local programs, domain boundaries, storage, and trust.", "text": "System architecture Boundaries before services Axiora should begin as two local programs sharing domain contracts: an Android study application and a command-line content compiler. No backend is required for the first release. Optional future AI adapters sit outside these core boundaries. They may propose content or feedback; they do not gain authority to mutate progress or install packages. Proposed module structure These are intended boundaries. The content, validation, compiler, learning-core, and initial JVM storage modules exist. The Android application does not yet exist. Module Responsibility Must not depend on `content-model` Typed identifiers, unit and concept structures, version parsing Android, database, UI, network `content-validation` Structural and semantic package rules, diagnostics Android UI, AI provider `learning-core` Session transitions, evaluation, evidence and review policy Android, file system, SQLite, network `storage` Transactions, persistence, migrations, content lookup UI components; implemented initially as a JVM SQLite JDBC adapter `android-app` Accessible screens, lifecycle, file selection, rendering Compiler generation internals `compiler-cli` Authoring input, normalization, validation, reproducible packaging Android Initially use Kotlin/JVM libraries, with Android-compatible dependencies for shared modules. Do not begin with Kotlin Multiplatform, a web client, or a distributed service architecture. Revisit extraction after a second supported client exists. Core application flow The UI sends typed commands such as `SubmitChoice`, `SaveDraft`, `RevealHint`, `RevealSolution`, `RecordReflection`, and `Advance`. The domain engine returns a new session state plus facts to persist. Storage commits them in one transaction before the UI presents the next durable state. The engine receives its clock, identifier generator, content lookup, and review policy through interfaces. A repeated command ID must return the original result without creating duplicate attempts or moving twice. Unsupported commands in a state produce a recoverable domain error rather than silently skipping content. Explanations do not imply submission. A choice must be explicitly submitted. A proof draft is saved frequently but does not become an assessment merely because the app closes. Content and learner storage Store immutable imported package revisions in app-private storage. Keep a local SQLite database for package registry, session checkpoints, proof drafts, attempts, observations, notes, and review tasks. Use the Android Storage Access Framework for user-selected import, export, and restore files. The database stores references to exact package and unit revisions. Old revisions stay available while active sessions or historical attempts refer to them. Content deduplication is optional; correctness and recovery take priority over saving a few files. Do not build a full event-sourced application. Use ordinary relational tables for current state with an append-only learning event history sufficient to explain observations and rebuild derived summaries. Session checkpoints and event rows are committed together. See [learner state](learner-state.md). Import transaction Copy the selected archive into a bounded staging area; do not trust the file extension. Inspect the archive before extraction: reject unsafe paths, links, duplicate names, excessive sizes, and unsupported entries. Read the manifest; check schema version and required capabilities. Verify inventory, raw-byte hashes, JSON structure, identifiers, graph references, and local dependencies. Show package identity, review claims, missing prerequisites, and any update consequences. On the learner's import action, atomically register the complete revision and make it available. On failure, retain the previous installed revision and remove only the new staging artifacts. No partial course may appear as installed. If a crash occurs between filesystem promotion and database registration, startup reconciliation must either complete the validated registration or remove the unregistered staged revision. It must never attach an incomplete payload to an installed record. Trust boundaries Input Trust level Required treatment Package text, equations, names, and metadata Untrusted authored data Escape text, restrict Markdown and math commands, enforce limits Archive file paths Untrusted file operations Normalize and reject traversal, links, collisions, and unlisted payloads Mathematical answer key Author claim Record provenance and review state; exact evaluation cannot validate the theorem Learner text Personal local data Preserve drafts, avoid logs containing content, upload only by explicit action Optional AI response Unverified suggestion Display source and limitations, never apply hidden grading or state mutations Hashes detect corruption and bind a manifest to its payload. They do not prove publisher identity: anyone can replace both. Cryptographic publisher signatures and a trust registry are deferred. The first app must label review status as a package author's claim rather than an authenticated endorsement. Mathematical rendering Use a renderer adapter, isolated from domain semantics. Mathematical source is restricted LaTeX stored as text. The initial supported subset must be established during a rendering spike; unsupported commands must fail author validation or render a readable source fallback with a diagnostic. The Android renderer may use a tightly restricted local WebView if the spike shows that a native renderer cannot meet notation needs. This is an implementation option, not a decision to trust package HTML. Bundle rendering resources in the app, disable remote requests and file navigation, sanitize content, and expose no JavaScript bridge to package data. Generated HTML must not be able to launch arbitrary intents or reach the network. Failure semantics Failure Result Evaluator unavailable Keep the response and mark it unassessed; do not guess Incompatible package schema Refuse installation; explain the required reader version Missing learning prerequisite Allow browsing, explain the gap, offer a remedial path if installed Invalid internal reference Reject the whole package revision Persistence failure Keep the current visible response; do not claim it was saved or advance durably Renderer failure Offer source text and a reportable diagnostic; preserve the session Clock moves backwards Retain event order; prevent automatic promotion and duplicate review tasks Consequences of this architecture The engine remains testable without Android, while the product gets native navigation, local storage, and lifecycle behavior. A JVM compiler reuses the package validator and reduces contract drift. The cost is maintaining a formal content boundary early. That cost is worthwhile because content imports and progress compatibility are harder to retrofit than a new screen."}, {"title": "Content contract", "url": "content.html", "description": "The proposed package format and execution semantics.", "text": "Content contract Contract status This is the proposed **Axiora content schema 0.1.0**. It is a design contract with a JSON example and an illustrative fixture. A strict Kotlin parser and shared validator are implemented for the compiler and package reader; the contract is checked in code rather than a standalone JSON Schema. This work completes milestone M1. The example uses runtime JSON directly. A high-level YAML authoring language is deferred until several manually authored units reveal what abstraction actually helps. This reduces the risk of designing a language before learning what teaching needs to express. Names and identity Use stable namespaced ASCII IDs, for example `axiora.ca.prime-vs-maximal`. IDs match `[a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:[.-][a-z0-9]+)*` and are at most 120 characters. Renaming explanatory prose does not change an ID. Each package and unit has a three-part numeric version such as `0.1.0`; prerelease syntax and version ranges are not supported initially. A unit ID identifies an educational contract; its version identifies one revision. Content hashes identify exact bytes. These are different responsibilities. Published revisions are immutable. The same package ID and version with a different manifest hash is a conflict, not an update. An identical import is idempotent. An update can change the installed default for new sessions; it cannot silently replace the revision pinned to an active session. Package layout `.axp` is a ZIP archive with UTF-8 JSON files, relative paths, and one root manifest. The [example directory](../../examples/prime-and-maximal/README.md) is the unpacked source of that layout. Its README is repository documentation and is not part of the archive inventory. Manifest fields All fields in the following table are required. Unknown fields are rejected in schema 0.1; future extensions need an explicit schema change. JSON duplicate object keys must be rejected before deserialization. Field Type Meaning `schema_version` version string Exact supported content contract `package_id` stable ID Identity across package updates `version` version string Immutable package revision `title` nonempty string Human-readable name `language` string Content language tag, initially `en` `license` string Distribution declaration; `UNLICENSED` means no distribution grant `requires_capabilities` unique string array Features the reader must support `conventions` nonempty string Mathematical assumptions applying to all units `concept_files` unique path array Concept documents in this package `units` nonempty object array Each contains `id`, `version`, and `path` `entry_units` nonempty ID array Suggested start points, referencing manifest units `review` object `status` and `note`, both strings `files` nonempty object array Each contains `path`, `bytes`, and lowercase `sha256` The payload inventory lists every file except `manifest.json`; the manifest cannot hash itself. No undeclared archive entries are allowed. The manifest digest stored by the importer is calculated separately over its raw bytes. Each declared concept or unit path must appear in the payload inventory. Payload paths cannot be shared between two unit records. SHA-256 values detect accidental or malicious byte changes relative to the manifest. They do not authenticate the author, publisher, or review claims. Review status is one of `draft`, `mathematically_reviewed`, or `pilot_reviewed`. The latter two must include reviewer identity, date, scope, and evidence in the note or a future structured review record. Until publisher authentication exists, the app presents these as author claims. The fixture remains `draft`. Compatibility and limits The 0.1 reader accepts only schema `0.1.0`. Later readers may explicitly support multiple versions; do not assume minor versions are readable. Required capabilities are an allowlist; an unknown required capability rejects import. Initial required capabilities are `activity.exposition.v1`, `activity.choice.v1`, `activity.proof.v1`, `activity.reflection.v1`, `activity.end.v1`, `evaluator.exact-choice.v1`, and `evaluator.self-review.v1`. These version the implemented semantics independently of a package's own version. Initial import limits are conservative policies, to be revised against representative courses: Archive size at most 20 MiB; total uncompressed payload at most 50 MiB. At most 500 files, each JSON file at most 2 MiB; manifest at most 256 KiB. At most 100 units and 500 activities per unit; JSON nesting depth at most 32. A Markdown field at most 100,000 Unicode characters; a title at most 200; an ID at most 120. UTF-8 JSON only, with bounded string and collection lengths and no duplicate object keys. ZIP entries must be regular files. Reject symlinks, encrypted entries, absolute paths, backslashes, empty path segments, `.` or `..` segments, NULs, and case-insensitive path collisions. Stop streaming extraction as soon as cumulative actual bytes exceed limits, regardless of declared archive sizes. Verify each declared byte count after extraction. The 0.1 payload is text only. Media, fonts, HTML, executable code, custom evaluators, external embeds, and network-dependent assets are unsupported. Broader asset formats require their own size, parsing, and rendering policies. Concepts and prerequisites A concept document has exactly `schema_version` and `concepts`. Each concept contains `id`, `title`, `definition_md`, `assumptions`, and `relations`. A relation contains `type` and `target`; type is `prerequisite`, `implies`, `equivalent`, or `generalizes`. `prerequisite` points from the dependent concept to the concept it requires. `implies` points from premise to consequence. `generalizes` points from the more general concept to the narrower one. `equivalent` is semantically symmetric, though one record is enough to declare it. Only prerequisite edges must form a directed acyclic graph. Equivalences naturally permit cycles and must not be confused with teaching order. Version 0.1 concept relation targets must be present in the same package. External prerequisite IDs may appear in a unit's `requires_concepts`, where they are advisory learning dependencies. They do not authorize missing executable or rendering dependencies. The reader may browse a unit with an unmet learning prerequisite but must show the gap. Do not merge concepts solely by matching titles. Different ring conventions or theorem hypotheses can require distinct IDs or explicit assumptions. Version 0.1 prevents importing conflicting definitions under the same ID into one active course view; it does not attempt universal ontology reconciliation. Learning unit fields All unit fields below are required. Units are independent compilation artifacts; collections are supplied by manifest entry points initially. A separate course/path document is deferred. Field Meaning `schema_version`, `id`, `version`, `title` Contract and identity `concept_ids` Nonempty unique list of locally defined concepts taught here `requires_concepts` Unique list of assumed prior concepts; local or external `objectives` Nonempty list of `{id, concept_id, skill, description}` `sources` Nonempty list of `{id, kind, citation, locator, rights}` `entry` ID of the first activity `activities` Nonempty mapping from local activity ID to activity Objective, source, and activity IDs are local stable IDs using the same syntax as global IDs. Objective `concept_id` must be in `concept_ids`. Skill keys are defined in [learning and assessment](pedagogy.md). `sources.kind` is `original` or `reference`; rights are a declaration, not an automated legal finding. Every activity must include a nonempty `source_ids` list pointing to local sources. Activity vocabulary The first implementation supports five types. Richer constructed responses, proof-step ordering, and parameterized problems are deferred. Type Required fields beyond `type` and `source_ids` Behavior `exposition` `content_md`, `next` Read material, then continue `choice` `prompt_md`, `options`, `evaluator`, `feedback`, `transitions` Select one answer and explicitly submit `proof` `prompt_md`, `hints`, `model_solution_md`, `rubric`, `evaluator`, `objective_id`, `next` Draft, reveal optional help, snapshot attempt, self-review `reflection` `prompt_md`, `next` Record optional free text and confidence `end` `summary_md` End the unit without inferring mastery `choice.options` is a list of at least two `{id, text_md}` records with unique local option IDs. Its evaluator is exactly `{type: \"exact_choice\", expected: <option ID>}`. `feedback` has `correct_md` and `incorrect_md`; `transitions` has `correct` and `incorrect`, both activity IDs. A choice may have one `evidence` object, with exactly `objective_id` and `assessment_family`. An evidence-bearing exact-choice activity must target an objective with skill `recall` or `recognition`. An assessment family is a globally namespaced stable ID shared by tasks that disclose essentially the same answer. Rewording or reordering choices does not create a fresh family. This keeps near-duplicate tasks from counting as independent demonstrations. `proof.hints` is an ordered array of `{id, content_md}`. `rubric` is a nonempty list of `{id, criterion_md}`. Its evaluator is exactly `{type: \"self_review\"}` and its objective must have skill `proof_construction` or `proof_comprehension`. Empty hint arrays are allowed. The runtime stores rubric responses separately; no correctness key is inferred from the model solution. Reflection text and confidence are optional learner input; the activity itself remains traversable with no answer. Confidence is `low`, `medium`, or `high`, or absent. It cannot produce checked correctness evidence. Execution semantics The activity graph must be finite and acyclic in 0.1, with every node reachable from `entry` and every path ending at an `end` activity. All transition targets are local. Authors express remediation by branching forward to additional activities, not by adding cycles. Retries are an engine affordance within the current choice activity. The learner sees feedback for a submission and may retry before selecting Continue; the branch is pinned to the **first submitted result**, so retries cannot bypass remediation. All submissions are retained. Back navigation is read-only history and does not resubmit or reset the active branch. Restarting a unit creates a new session, not a rewrite of old attempts. A proof can be saved and resumed without revealing a solution. Continue requires an explicit submitted attempt or a deliberate “Skip for now” action. Skipping records no correctness evidence. Revealing the solution snapshots the current draft and records assistance; continuing records a self-review only when the learner actually supplies rubric responses. Evidence is recorded when an answer is durably submitted, not when the next screen loads. `end` records session completion only. The app must preserve the current response if persistence fails. Markdown and mathematics Allow paragraphs, headings below the activity title, emphasis, lists, code spans, code blocks, and tables. Disable raw HTML, images, remote embeds, and executable links. Version 0.1 content contains no clickable links; source citations are plain text. Display LaTeX source using a bundled renderer's approved subset and a readable fallback. The documentation website has ordinary navigation links; its Markdown rendering rules are separate from imported course content. Never reuse a permissive documentation renderer as a package trust boundary. Update compatibility Patch revisions may correct wording without changing keys, objectives, or mathematical meaning. Any answer-key, hypothesis, objective, rubric-meaning, or assessment-family change requires at least a minor unit version bump and a migration review. Major versions mark structural or educational incompatibility. These are author obligations, not something semantic version parsing can prove. Version 0.1 transfers no derived evidence automatically to a different unit version. Old attempts remain visible and pinned to their original content. A future explicitly reviewed compatibility map may allow selective evidence reuse. Stable IDs preserve association; they do not justify reinterpreting old work."}, {"title": "Learner state", "url": "learner-state.html", "description": "Attempts, persistence, versioning, backup, and review.", "text": "Learner state and review Preserve the work before summarizing it Proof drafts, submitted attempts, opened hints, and learner notes are primary records. Progress indicators and recommendations are derived views. If a summary is wrong or a policy changes, it should be possible to rebuild it without losing the learner's actual work. Learner data never belongs inside an installed content package. Removing a course from the active library must not silently delete its attempts or notes. Proposed local records Record Important fields Lifetime `package_revision` Package ID, version, manifest hash, storage location, import time, active-library flag Immutable installed identity `session` ID, exact package/unit revision, active activity, first-result branch, state, checkpoint Mutable checkpoint `draft` Session, activity, text, revision counter, modified time Mutable, retained until explicit removal `attempt` ID, command ID, activity, sequence, response, outcome, method, assistance snapshot, readable context snapshot Immutable submission `learning_event` Monotonic database sequence, event ID, type, payload version, occurrence time Append-only explanation history `observation` Attempt, objective, skill, family, evidence-policy version Immutable evidence input `objective_summary` Objective and unit revision, descriptive state, supporting attempts, policy version Rebuildable projection `review_task` Objective and unit revision, due date, interval stage, status Rebuildable recommendation `note` Stable content anchor, optional exact revision, text, timestamps Learner-owned editable work An `attempt` must retain enough of the assessed prompt, selected option, evaluator key revision, and rubric context to interpret the result if the original package is removed. Full old package revisions should remain while referenced, but a bounded prompt snapshot provides a second layer of historical readability. Do not store one mutable “score” row as the only record of a person's learning. Session state machine An active activity has type-specific substates. For a choice: awaiting selection, ready to submit, feedback visible, ready to continue. For a proof: drafting, comparing, reflecting, ready to continue. The first submission fixes a choice branch even when later retries succeed. Pause records a checkpoint. Restart creates a new session. Back navigation exposes snapshots without moving the durable checkpoint. Completed sessions stay completed; revisiting creates new work. Atomicity and duplicate commands Each user command has a generated unique ID. A transaction writes its attempt or event, updates the session checkpoint, and updates or invalidates projections together. A uniqueness constraint on command ID prevents double taps, process retries, or lifecycle restoration from producing duplicate learning events. Draft saving uses a monotonically increasing revision counter, not wall-clock last-write-wins. An older autosave cannot overwrite a newer draft. Display an unsaved indicator until the transaction commits. On a storage failure, keep the current text on screen and offer retry or explicit copying/export; do not advance and hope it was saved. Android lifecycle termination can happen without a final callback. Save after edits with a short debounce and on explicit actions; do not rely solely on a pause callback. Exact debounce and retention limits are implementation tuning parameters. Events and projections Initial event types are `session_started`, `choice_submitted`, `hint_revealed`, `solution_revealed`, `proof_submitted`, `rubric_recorded`, `activity_skipped`, `reflection_recorded`, and `session_completed`. Draft edits are stored as drafts rather than a keystroke event stream. Every event includes its session and content revision, a payload schema version, and the database sequence. Timestamps support review scheduling; sequence establishes order when clocks disagree. Domain commands emit events only after valid state transitions. The app may recompute summaries after a policy upgrade. It must retain previous attempt outcomes, provenance, and assessment methods. An AI opinion added later cannot overwrite a historical self-review or exact-choice result. Initial review scheduler Use a transparent interval ladder: **1, 3, 7, 14, and 30 days**. This is an initial product heuristic, not an optimal learning claim. A task records its stage and policy version. The first eligible, unassisted, first-submission exact-choice success creates a review for the next local calendar day. At a due review, an eligible success from a different assessment family advances one stage and schedules from the completion date. Practice before the due date does not advance the ladder. An eligible first-submission error resets the stage and schedules the next day. A hinted answer, retry, skipped activity, technical failure, or solution-assisted response does not advance the ladder. Offer practice or keep the task pending without creating an immediate repetition loop. A self-reviewed proof offers a learner-chosen reminder, defaulting to the next day. It never automatically advances a checked-skill ladder. At the last stage, successful due reviews remain on a 30-day interval; no content is declared permanently learned. Deduplicate tasks by objective, exact unit revision, and policy version. A unit with no fresh assessment family can still be practiced, but the app must explain that no new independent check is available. The first course needs at least two families per checked objective to avoid this dead end. Use the device's local date for presentation and due-day calculations. Keep UTC timestamps and the recorded zone offset for history. If the clock moves backwards, preserve sequence order and postpone automatic evidence promotion until timestamps are coherent. A large forward jump may make tasks due, but must not produce automatic success or a storm of duplicate tasks. The `demonstrated` rule additionally requires at least 24 elapsed hours and distinct local study dates, as defined in [assessment policy](pedagogy.md). Due-day scheduling and evidence eligibility are intentionally separate. Recommendation policy Offer at most three clear actions: resume unfinished work, review a due objective, or study the next available unit. Explain each recommendation in plain language: “You used a hint last time” or “This idea is due for review.” The learner can choose another topic. Prerequisite gaps create a visible suggestion, not an absolute navigation lock. Course authors may suggest an order but cannot require the app to conceal definitions until a score threshold is reached. Updating and removing content Active sessions pin exact revisions. Installing a newer course revision affects new sessions only. Offer “Continue existing work” and “Start updated unit,” showing that historical observations remain with the old revision. A derived summary is never silently carried across unit versions in 0.1. Uninstalling content makes it unavailable for new sessions. Historical attempts and notes remain readable through their snapshots, and old revisions needed by active work remain retained. Explicit learner-data deletion is a separate action showing the scope. Garbage collection must not erase referenced revisions or orphan learner work. Backup and restore Version 0.1 should export a user-selected archive containing a versioned JSON learner snapshot and retained content references. It excludes textbook files, credentials, transient caches, and optional AI secrets. Include the database schema/export version, all draft and attempt IDs, and package hashes. Show that proof text and notes are personal data before export. Use an application-level serialization, not a raw live SQLite copy. Take a consistent snapshot in a transaction. Backup encryption is deferred; clearly label the file as readable personal data and let the user choose its destination. Do not upload it automatically. For the first release, restore is permitted only into an empty learner profile. Validate limits, versions, referential integrity, and IDs in staging; commit atomically. Reject a nonempty-profile restore with a clear explanation rather than attempting an unspecified merge. Missing packages do not invalidate readable learner history; mark the content unavailable and offer reimport by hash. Cross-device synchronization and merge conflict resolution are deferred. Phase 4 JVM persistence format The implemented adapter uses SQLite schema 2 and application backup format 2. These versions are independent of content schema 0.1. A backup is one UTF-8 JSON document with exactly `format`, `version`, and `tables`; it is not a live database copy or a package archive. The format identifier is `axiora-learner-backup`. Tables contain exact record columns, BLOBs use base64 strings, and numeric fields use integers. The adapter bounds input to 64 MiB, 250,000 records per table, and 2,000,000 characters per text field. Duplicate keys, trailing JSON, unknown formats/tables/columns, invalid field types, and relational constraint failures are rejected before commit. For example, a format-2 package record adds `\"active\": 0` when removed from the active library; an attempt adds `\"context_snapshot\": \"Prompt: …; selected option: …; key revision: …; rubric: …\"`. `StudyRepository` requires the application to supply bounded readable context from the pinned package, because the domain model contains identifiers rather than teaching prose. `attemptHistory` reads the response, method, assistance, and context without loading package files. Resume still requires the exact pinned unit through `UnitLookup`. Schema 1 migrates transactionally to schema 2 without rewriting primary records. Existing packages start active. Older attempts receive the explicit text `Context unavailable in schema 1`; migration cannot reconstruct a prompt that was never saved. Format-1 backups remain accepted with the same defaults. Format 2 is refused by the older format-1 reader. The [saved schema-1 fixture](../../storage/src/test/resources/schema-v1.sql) includes attempts, drafts, events, observations, summaries, review tasks, notes, and a durable command receipt for compatibility checks. Library removal sets the active flag and blocks new sessions, retaining archives, existing sessions, attempts, and notes. Reconciliation preserves that flag; an explicit successful reimport reactivates the revision. No automatic content garbage collection or learner-data deletion is implemented. Recovery deletes abandoned import staging files and registers validated promoted archives left by a terminated process; a failed registration keeps those bytes for retry. Run reconciliation at startup before accepting imports, using one installer at a time. Android lifecycle wiring remains Phase 5. Restore validates and inserts records in one transaction into an empty profile and returns references whose archive cannot be validated against the recorded identity and hash. It preserves the original content references, command receipts, and personal work even when all packages are missing. Package bytes are excluded; the application must reimport unavailable packages and resolve exact revisions before resuming. UI file selection, privacy notices, and saved/unsaved indicators remain Android work. Privacy defaults No account, background network calls, analytics collection, advertising identifiers, or automatic crash uploads are needed. Local diagnostics should contain codes and content IDs, not proof text or textbook passages. A support export should preview its contents and omit personal work unless selected. Future AI tutoring must require an explicit per-request send action showing the chosen prompt and relevant excerpts. That action cannot grant indefinite access to the notebook or background upload permission."}, {"title": "Authoring & compilation", "url": "compiler.html", "description": "From reviewed units to reproducible packages.", "text": "Authoring and compilation Start with authored content The first compiler should validate and package manually authored runtime JSON. It should not start by reading arbitrary PDFs. A reliable hand-authored unit gives the team a reference for what any later generator must produce. The compiler is a local CLI, sharing contract types and semantic validation with the package archive reader. An Android reader does not exist yet; it is intended to reuse this validator. Python scripts in this repository build and check documentation; they are not the production compiler. The content pipeline Stage Input Output Responsibility Author Learning objectives, original prose, permitted references Editable units and concepts Human author, optionally assisted by AI Parse UTF-8 JSON files Typed data or precise syntax errors Strict parser Validate Typed units and concepts Diagnostics with paths and rule IDs Shared validator Link Validated units and concept IDs Resolved graph and capability inventory Compiler Review Content and diagnostics Scoped review record Human mathematical and teaching reviewers Package Selected immutable revisions `.axp` archive and build report Deterministic packager Compilation is independent of packaging. A cache may reuse a unit only if its raw content hash, referenced concept definitions, compiler version, and validation policy are unchanged. The same text under changed assumptions must be revalidated. Proposed CLI surface The Phase 1 and Phase 2 CLI commands are implemented in the local Gradle distribution: `validate` exits 0 for no blocking errors and 1 for invalid content. Invocation or I/O failures exit 2. Human-readable output goes to stderr when a machine-readable JSON result is requested. `pack` refuses a preexisting different output unless an explicit overwrite flag is supplied. Validation never rewrites source files. An authoring directory may contain a README, but the packager includes only the manifest and its declared payloads. A reader inspecting the final archive must reject undeclared files. Diagnostic contract A diagnostic contains `code`, `severity`, `file`, `path`, `message`, and an optional `suggestion`. JSON paths identify fields precisely; a future YAML parser may add line and column positions. Proposed diagnostic groups: `AXC1xx`: syntax, schema, unsupported version or capability. `AXC2xx`: IDs, references, graph reachability, prerequisites. `AXC3xx`: evaluator keys, objectives, evidence or assistance semantics. `AXC4xx`: package inventory, paths, byte limits, hashes. `AXC5xx`: provenance, review claims, content quality warnings. Schema errors, invalid references, unsupported capabilities, missing answer keys, and unsafe paths block packaging. Suspected teaching weakness may be a warning during drafting but block a pilot release through its release checklist. The compiler must not label a theorem mathematically false merely because a language model objects to it. Validation layers **Structural:** exact field shapes, unique JSON keys, bounded lengths, enumerated evaluator types, compatible schema version. **Referential:** local IDs exist, objective concepts are taught by the unit, source references resolve, manifest unit records match their payload identity. **Control flow:** entry exists, targets exist, all nodes are reachable, activity graph has no cycles, each path terminates, concept prerequisites have no cycles. **Assessment:** answer keys refer to actual options, exact-choice evidence targets recall or recognition, proof objectives use self-review, and assessment family IDs remain explicit. Validate hints and rubrics as content structures, not as mathematical proofs. **Packaging:** permitted paths, declared inventory, sizes, raw-byte hashes, capability coverage, and supported content encodings. **Mathematics and teaching:** human review checks truth, hypotheses, notation, difficulty, useful feedback, fair distractors, and objective alignment. Passing the earlier layers does not imply passing this layer. Reproducible packaging Use lexicographically ordered archive paths, fixed ZIP timestamps, regular-file entries, and UTF-8 JSON with object keys sorted recursively (array order is retained). Hash final payload bytes after serialization, then write the manifest with a sorted payload inventory. For reproducible first-release archives, use ZIP STORE with no compression; compression may be introduced under a pinned toolchain later. The 50 MiB uncompressed limit still applies. The adjacent build report records tool and policy identifiers, package revision, manifest and archive hashes, payload count, and uncompressed byte count; it contains no wall-clock timestamp. `pack` refuses an existing output unless `--overwrite` is supplied and requires the archive output to be outside the authoring directory. It validates the source first and includes only payloads declared in the manifest inventory. `inspect` checks bounded ZIP metadata and streamed bytes, stages the package temporarily, and runs the shared content validator. It returns a validated revision descriptor; installation remains a later storage responsibility. Package container options The package must be one offline-importable, immutable revision with a manifest, bounded payloads, compatibility checks, and byte-integrity checks. The current contract uses a ZIP container with JSON payload files. This is a selected implementation format, not a product invariant. Option Advantages Costs and risks Fit for the current release ZIP with separate JSON payload files One portable file; separately named concepts and units; per-payload sizes and hashes; readers can validate each bounded file; ordinary ZIP tooling can inspect the container. Requires careful handling of paths, duplicate entries, symlinks, metadata, and expanded sizes. ZIP STORE avoids compression variability but gives no size reduction. Current choice. It matches the manifest inventory and keeps each runtime payload separately addressable. One JSON package envelope One portable file; one parser and one bounded document; no archive paths, local headers, or expansion behavior. Deterministic serialization is straightforward. The reader parses a single document; selective reading is less direct. Payload boundaries and hashes need a canonical representation inside the envelope. Strong candidate while content remains small and text-only. Simpler than ZIP if independent payload addressing has little practical value. SQLite package One portable file; generated by the compiler; indexed reads and relational constraints are available without manual SQL authoring. Makes the database schema and SQLite compatibility part of the package contract. Byte-for-byte reproducibility and validation require a controlled database build and read policy. The reader must treat the database as hostile input and forbid executable database features. Viable if package size, indexed access, or relational queries justify the database contract. Manual authoring is not a concern because the compiler can create it. The current choice rests on a package contract organized around separately named payload files and per-file inventory, bounds, and hashes. It does not rest on JSON being the authoring format. A JSON envelope should be reconsidered if all content must be loaded together and the file boundaries provide little reader or review value. SQLite should be reconsidered if selective access or relational queries become a measured need. ZIP’s security checks remain a real maintenance cost. A compiler build report lives outside the archive. It records tool version, source hashes, policy version, diagnostics, and the final archive hash. Avoid wall-clock timestamps inside reproducible payloads. A repeated build of unchanged sources with the same compiler and policy must be byte-identical. Incremental generation later When AI authoring is introduced, accept a bounded source scope: selected passages, a concept, or one objective. Produce a draft unit and proposed concept changes. Never silently merge those changes into a shared concept catalog. Record source edition and locator, extraction quality, model/provider identifier, prompt template version, generation settings, and review outcomes in the authoring workspace. A generated unit remains `draft` until reviewed. Do not put private prompts, textbook scans, or provider credentials in learner packages. Copyright and licensing must be resolved before publishing source-derived material. Source access is not itself permission to redistribute text or generated derivatives. A future source-ingestion tool should distinguish private authoring workspace files from distributable content. Why postpone a YAML DSL? The essential language is the semantic model: activities, evaluations, transitions, evidence, and provenance. JSON is sufficient to discover whether that model can teach. A second syntax is useful only when repeated authoring work reveals avoidable duplication or difficulty. Reopen this decision after three substantially different reviewed units: a definition with examples, a theorem with proof analysis, and a proof technique applied in multiple settings. Preserve one normalized runtime contract regardless of authoring syntax."}, {"title": "Study experience", "url": "experience.html", "description": "The intended Android flows and recovery states.", "text": "Study experience A quiet workspace with a clear next step The Android experience should prioritize reading, mathematical notation, and recoverable writing. It should feel comfortable during a long proof attempt and remain useful for a five-minute review. This is a functional interaction design, not an approved visual mockup or finished application. Use native Android navigation, accessibility, text selection, and lifecycle conventions. Avoid a dense graph as the first screen. A map becomes useful only after the learner has enough concepts to navigate. Library and home With no content installed, explain what a course package is and offer **Import course**. Do not show an empty dashboard full of invented metrics. An imported course shows its title, assumptions, prerequisites, version, and review claim. With work in progress, home prioritizes **Continue learning**, followed by due reviews and the course library. A course can be browsed by unit or concept. Notes and unfinished proof attempts remain reachable without restarting a lesson. Do not show a single mastery ring or a completion percentage as a proxy for understanding. Prefer concrete language: “Two independent recognition checks,” “Proof attempt awaiting comparison,” and “Review due today.” Import flow Select a local `.axp` file through the system file picker. Show validation progress with cancellation before installation commits. Present identity, conventions, review status, prerequisite gaps, and update effects. Offer **Import course** or **Keep existing version** where relevant. On success, show the available starting units. On failure, preserve the existing library and show a diagnostic with a recovery action. Example error: “This course needs a newer content reader. Your existing work is safe. Install a compatible app version or choose another package.” Avoid exposing stack traces in learner-facing flows. Guided activity Present one primary task at a time with its objective accessible nearby. A single-choice question uses native selection semantics and a separate **Submit answer** action. Feedback explains the mathematical issue and offers **Try again** or **Continue**. The first submitted result determines the authored branch. The learner can inspect relevant definitions; opening that help records assistance if the task is being assessed. UI navigation controls must not accidentally reveal the correct answer or resubmit an attempt. History is readable without mutating the active checkpoint. A learner may leave a session and return to the same selected answer or draft. Proof workspace Use a text editor with readable LaTeX source and an optional preview. Provide common mathematical symbols, undo, selection, and copy. A working save indicator is more important than a sophisticated equation keyboard. The proof prompt remains available while writing. On a phone, use a clearly labeled prompt panel rather than squeezing two columns onto the screen; on larger devices, a split workspace may be appropriate. Validate the layout with real long equations and long proof drafts. Actions: **Save and leave:** preserve the draft and return home. **Show a hint:** reveal the next hint and record it without changing the draft. **Compare with solution:** snapshot the current attempt, reveal the model solution, and show the rubric. **Skip for now:** record the skip and continue without inventing a score. During comparison, keep the learner's argument visible or one tap away. The rubric supports **Met**, **Not met**, and **Unsure**, plus a reminder that an alternative proof can be valid. Revision creates a new attempt linked to the earlier one; it does not overwrite history. Reviews A review explains why it is offered and what will be checked. A fresh assessment family is preferred over repeating the exact same question. If no fresh check exists, label the task as practice. Learners can defer reviews or choose a smaller session. Overdue work should be grouped into a manageable choice rather than a punitive backlog. No streak loss, countdown pressure, or synthetic reward is required. Essential states State Learner sees Recovery No courses Brief explanation and import action Select a package Missing prerequisite Named assumed concept Read a reminder or browse available remedial units Draft saving Honest saving/saved status Stay on task Save failed Current text retained, unsaved status Retry or explicitly copy/export Unsupported formula Readable LaTeX source and diagnostic Continue or report the issue Content update Old and new versions, consequences Continue pinned work or start the updated unit No fresh review task Practice label Practice or select another topic No network Normal core behavior No connectivity prompt is needed Accessibility and inclusion Support system font scaling, sufficient contrast, keyboard focus, TalkBack labels, and large touch targets. Never rely on color alone for outcomes. Announce save failures and assessment feedback accessibly without stealing focus from the editor. Mathematical accessibility needs a dedicated spike: spoken expressions, selectable source, reading order, and line navigation must be evaluated with real assistive technology. Rendering a visual formula does not satisfy that requirement. Provide plain-language descriptions where practical and retain access to the mathematical source. The first content language is English. Keep interface strings localizable and store the package language, but defer translated curriculum and right-to-left notation combinations until they can be tested. Do not put hard-coded English feedback in evaluator logic; feedback belongs to the content or localized UI. Optional AI interaction later An optional **Discuss this attempt** action can prepare a preview containing the prompt, learner-selected proof text, and selected course references. The learner sees the destination provider and sends deliberately. They can edit or remove excerpts. Show the resulting feedback as an unverified external suggestion. It may ask questions or point out possible gaps. It must not change the saved attempt, correctness method, or mastery summary automatically. The core session remains usable if the provider is unavailable."}, {"title": "Delivery & verification", "url": "delivery.html", "description": "Milestones, acceptance scenarios, and risks.", "text": "Delivery and verification Build one complete learning loop Milestones are ordered by risk and evidence, not estimated calendar dates. There is no implementation team, budget, or delivery date established. M0, M1, and M2 are complete; Phase 4 durable JVM storage passes its acceptance gate; Android work remains Phase 5. See the [verified implementation status](../../IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md). Milestone Deliverable Exit condition M0 — Design baseline Handbook, repository guidance, example fixture, documentation checks Human and AI editions agree; example follows its documented subset M1 — Executable content contract Kotlin types, strict schema, shared validator, deterministic packager Valid fixture packages; invalid and hostile inputs fail with useful diagnostics M2 — Headless study loop Domain engine, command processing, attempts, evidence and review policy A complete branching unit runs deterministically without Android or network M3 — Android vertical slice Import, lesson, proof editor, local persistence, backup/restore A learner completes, closes, resumes, reviews, and restores work offline M4 — Reviewed course and pilot Complete course slice with fresh assessment families, reviewed mathematics, usability sessions Findings identify learning and interface weaknesses; blocking data-loss and assessment issues resolved M5 — Authoring assistance Incremental AI draft generation and provenance, only if justified Drafts are reviewable and validated; no automatic mathematical certification Optional conversational tutoring follows evidence of need. It is not a gate for M1–M4. For actionable tasks and phase gates, follow the [phased implementation plan](../../IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md). It expands these milestones without changing their contracts. First implementation task Start M1 with the current JSON fixture and strict models for five activity types. Implement structural and semantic validation and a CLI that reports errors. Add hostile archive tests before building a general authoring pipeline. Then use that same validator behind Android import. The fixture is deliberately small. Expand it into three units before freezing the contract: prerequisite recognition, the distinction and counterexample, and proof construction plus a later fresh check. Do not infer a full course from one successful fixture. Acceptance scenarios ID Scenario Required observable result C01 Import the same package twice One immutable revision, no duplicated progress C02 Import a different payload with the same ID/version Clear conflict; installed revision preserved C03 Encounter traversal, symlink, duplicate filename/key, oversized expansion, or unknown capability Reject before unsafe extraction or partial installation L01 Submit a wrong choice, retry correctly, then continue Both attempts retained; first-result remediation branch preserved L02 Tap submit twice or replay the same command after restart One durable submission and one transition L03 Finish a unit by skipping a proof Completion recorded, no fabricated proof correctness P01 Reveal a hint or solution before answering Assistance retained; no independent evidence credit P02 Self-review a proof with all rubric items marked met Method remains self-review; no machine-checked proof claim D01 Force-stop while writing, reopen, then revise Last committed draft restored; saved/unsaved status truthful D02 Install a revised unit while an old session exists Old session remains pinned; new work can use the new revision D03 Uninstall a course with notes and attempts Historical work remains readable; explicit deletion is separate R01 Return for a fresh due check Review stage changes only on eligible first-submission evidence R02 Change device clock or zone No duplicate tasks, reordered history, or automatic success B01 Export and restore into an empty profile Attempts, drafts, notes, and references retained; missing packages identified B02 Restore into a nonempty profile or import a corrupt backup Refuse safely; current learner data unchanged A01 Use large fonts, TalkBack, and keyboard navigation Core actions and mathematical source remain available O01 Enable airplane mode after installation All core import, study, draft, review, and backup flows work Test strategy when implementation begins Use domain tests for graph traversal, retry semantics, evidence eligibility, review dates, clock anomalies, and command idempotence. Use property-based or generated cases for bounded graph validation and path normalization where they find meaningful edge cases. Use importer integration tests with malformed archives, transactional crash points, hash mismatches, and incomplete filesystem/database promotion. Use real database migration tests with saved attempts and drafts from previous schema versions. Use Android integration tests for import, force-stop recovery, content updates, storage errors, backup/restore, and navigation. Run formula rendering and accessibility checks on representative phones and tablets. UI screenshots cannot establish correctness of learning state. Use deterministic build checks to confirm byte-identical archives for unchanged inputs. Mathematical review remains human work and should be recorded alongside test results rather than implied by a green CI run. Checks available in this repository The first checks generated HTML freshness. The second verifies local links/fragments and a limited set of fixture invariants: payload hashes, identity, supported activity shapes, references, reachability, cycles, and evaluator/objective pairing. It does not implement the full import contract, archive inspection, a complete schema validator, Android execution, or mathematical proof checking. Risks and planned reductions Risk Reduction before expansion DSL expresses pages but cannot teach reasoning Manually author different unit types before introducing syntax sugar Proof feedback creates false confidence Keep self-review explicit; use human-reviewed examples and rubrics Formula rendering is inaccessible or fragile Run a focused notation and assistive-technology spike before UI scale-up Content updates corrupt learner history Pin revisions and test updates with historical records Course creation consumes more effort than expected Measure authoring and review time per unit in the pilot Evidence model rewards guessing or repeated exposure Distinct task families, first-submission policy, delayed independent checks Large imported archives exhaust storage or memory Bounded parsing, streaming limits, staged transactions, hostile fixtures Optional AI expands privacy or correctness risk Explicit send preview, adapter boundary, no authority over grades Questions to settle at the right milestone Before M1: exact Kotlin build layout, JSON schema tooling, and CLI distribution method. Before M3: Android minimum version, renderer, SQLite adapter, backup serialization details, and supported devices. Before external distribution: software license, course rights, name availability, and any publisher verification model. These are real open decisions. They do not justify delaying the domain contract or creating a cloud service prematurely."}, {"title": "Decision log", "url": "decisions.html", "description": "Rationale, tradeoffs, and conditions for revisiting choices.", "text": "Decision log These decisions establish the proposed design baseline dated 16 November 2026. “Adopted for design” means the documentation follows the decision; it does not mean the relevant software exists. ADR-001 — Offline core, optional AI **Status:** adopted for design. **Problem:** Advanced mathematics benefits from dialogue, but a network-dependent tutor introduces cost, availability, privacy, and correctness concerns into every session. **Decision:** All required learning flows operate locally. AI authoring and tutoring are optional adapters with no authority to certify proofs or mutate learning evidence automatically. **Tradeoff:** The first app cannot respond intelligently to every unexpected argument. It compensates with reviewed feedback, model solutions, rubrics, and preserved work. **Reopen when:** Learner evidence identifies a recurring need that structured content cannot adequately address, with a viable privacy and assessment design. ADR-002 — Separate concepts, units, packages, and learner records **Status:** adopted for design. **Problem:** Textbook chapter order is not a reliable unit of learning or updating. **Decision:** Concepts describe knowledge; units describe teaching; packages distribute immutable revisions; learner records retain personal work independently. **Tradeoff:** More identifiers and explicit linking are needed. Content cannot simply be one mutable chapter file. **Reopen when:** A concrete content case cannot be represented without redundant or ambiguous identity. Preserve historical anchors in any migration. ADR-003 — JSON runtime first, authoring DSL later **Status:** adopted for design; revises the original YAML-first proposal. **Problem:** Designing two formats before writing real units risks encoding assumptions that do not survive teaching practice. **Decision:** Author the first units directly in strict runtime JSON with Markdown/LaTeX fields. Add a higher-level YAML or other authoring representation after repeated reviewed examples show the need. **Tradeoff:** JSON is less pleasant for long prose. Early verbosity buys fewer semantic layers and a simpler compatibility story. **Reopen when:** Three materially different reviewed unit types reveal stable repetition or authoring friction. A new authoring language must compile to the same normalized model. ADR-004 — Kotlin domain, native Android, JVM CLI **Status:** proposed implementation baseline; no dependencies selected. **Problem:** The reader and compiler should agree on validation and learning semantics without requiring a backend. **Decision:** Share Android-compatible Kotlin domain and validation libraries between a Compose Android client and a JVM command-line compiler. Keep storage, UI, and provider adapters outside the core. **Tradeoff:** This does not immediately support a browser client. Cross-platform extraction is deferred until a second real client exists. **Reopen when:** A validated second-platform requirement or team constraint outweighs the cost of a shared Kotlin/JVM foundation. ADR-005 — Small acyclic activity graphs **Status:** adopted for schema 0.1. **Problem:** Unrestricted branching and loops make author validation, resumption, and evidence interpretation harder. **Decision:** A unit is a finite directed acyclic graph. Retries are bounded by engine interaction semantics, and review creates separate work. Five activity types are supported initially. **Tradeoff:** Some sophisticated tutoring strategies require duplicate authored nodes or separate units. **Reopen when:** Reviewed courses require a specific cyclic pattern that cannot be expressed clearly otherwise. Define termination and persistence semantics before adding it. ADR-006 — Proof self-review is not automatic grading **Status:** adopted for design. **Problem:** A plausible-looking proof, or a learner's confidence in it, does not establish correctness. **Decision:** First-release proofs use model solutions and rubrics. Store self-review as its own method with an unassessed correctness outcome. Exact-choice evidence can support recall/recognition only. **Tradeoff:** Proof mastery remains a descriptive record requiring further judgment. The UI cannot offer a universal mastery score. **Reopen when:** A constrained formal or symbolic evaluator can support a precisely defined task family. Keep verification method explicit and preserve older records. ADR-007 — Immutable revisions and conservative migration **Status:** adopted for design. **Problem:** Changing an answer key or hypothesis can make historical scores misleading. **Decision:** Pin sessions and attempts to exact content revisions. A new unit version inherits no derived evidence automatically. Retain old work and snapshots; compatible IDs alone are insufficient. **Tradeoff:** Updates can require rechecking known material and retaining old content bytes. **Reopen when:** A reviewed compatibility map can justify selective evidence reuse with explainable, reversible transformations. ADR-008 — Transparent review rules before statistical adaptation **Status:** adopted as a pilot policy, not an efficacy claim. **Problem:** An elaborate mastery model can hide weak evidence behind a precise-looking percentage. **Decision:** Use skill-specific descriptive states, distinct assessment families, first-submission evidence, and a simple interval ladder. Store policy versions and retain observations. **Tradeoff:** The rules are coarse and may understate genuine knowledge. They are inspectable and easy to revise after observing learners. **Reopen when:** Pilot evidence shows systematic misclassification or a tested alternative provides better calibration. Recompute projections without rewriting attempts. ADR-009 — Local records and explicit export **Status:** adopted for first release. **Problem:** Synchronization creates account, privacy, conflict, and operational requirements before the offline loop is proven. **Decision:** Use local storage and explicit backup/restore. Restore into an empty profile only. No background telemetry or cloud service. **Tradeoff:** Multi-device use and automatic disaster recovery are absent. Users must export their work deliberately. **Reopen when:** Learners demonstrate a need for synchronization and its ownership, encryption, conflict, and recovery model has been designed. ADR-010 — One source for human and AI documentation **Status:** implemented for documentation. **Problem:** Independently written HTML and Markdown drift and can give implementers contradictory instructions. **Decision:** Canonical Markdown generates static HTML, navigation, and a local search index. Commit HTML so reading needs no toolchain. Check links and generated freshness. **Tradeoff:** Presentation changes belong in the generator and shared assets. Source-only internal details are not separately maintained. **Reopen when:** A documentation requirement cannot be supported by the current generator without disproportionate complexity. Preserve one canonical source and offline reading. ADR-011 — ZIP container for the first package reader **Status:** adopted for the current implementation; alternatives remain open. **Problem:** A course revision needs one offline-importable artifact, bounded payloads, a manifest inventory, and byte-integrity checks. **Decision:** Package separately named JSON payload files with a root manifest in a ZIP archive. Use ZIP STORE and deterministic metadata for the first reader. The compiler and reader reject unsafe archive paths and metadata before validating staged payloads. **Tradeoff:** ZIP adds path, metadata, duplicate-entry, and expansion checks. A single JSON envelope could remove archive handling for small packages. A compiler-generated SQLite package is also viable and avoids manual database authoring, but it makes the database schema and SQLite compatibility part of the content contract. **Reopen when:** Package scale and access patterns show that separate files provide little value, or when selective indexed reads justify a database package. Compare reader complexity and compatibility costs against the ZIP implementation before changing the contract. See [package container options](compiler.md#package-container-options). ADR-012 — Relational learner records and durable command receipts **Status:** adopted for the Phase 4 JVM storage adapter; Android integration remains unverified. **Problem:** The headless engine can produce a new checkpoint, attempts, and events, but process restart currently loses them and replaying a command can apply it twice. **Decision:** Store learner records in a versioned SQLite schema. The JVM adapter uses Xerial SQLite JDBC 3.54.1.0. A command transaction checks its durable receipt before invoking the transition callback, then commits the checkpoint, optional draft, attempts, events, projection invalidations, and versioned result payload together. Replays return the original payload. Package revisions are unique by package ID and version; a different manifest hash is a conflict. **Phase 4 completion:** Schema 2 adds active-library membership and readable attempt context. Removal retains content and learner work; reimport reactivates explicitly. The application supplies prompt/key/rubric snapshots to the repository. Schema-1 records migrate with explicit unavailable-context text rather than invented historical detail. Backup format 2 accepts format-1 records with those defaults. Failed database registration leaves a validated promoted archive for startup reconciliation; exception and hard JVM-termination tests cover recovery. The phase introduces no evidence transfer across revisions. **Tradeoff:** Result and checkpoint payload encoding remain application responsibilities, and this initial JDBC adapter does not establish Android runtime compatibility. The Android storage driver and lifecycle behavior must be selected and verified during the Android phase while preserving the transaction and schema invariants. **Reopen when:** Android compatibility or migration evidence requires a different driver or schema. Keep durable command identity, atomic primary-record writes, and immutable package identity across any replacement."}];
